  "id": "Compound::DB00862 - Gene::5148",
  "verbalization": "Vardenafil is implicated in the regulation of 3',5'-cyclic-gmp phosphodiesterase activity through its interaction with Pde11a and Pde10a, which are both involved in the same biological process. This suggests that Vardenafil exerts its effects by modulating the activity of phosphodiesterases, a class of enzymes critical for the degradation of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP). The evidence indicates that Vardenafil may influence Pde6g through a shared pathway involving the regulation of cGMP hydrolysis. The integration of multiple paths highlights a potential mechanism where Vardenafil indirectly affects Pde6g by targeting related phosphodiesterases, thereby influencing cGMP levels. The consistency in the involvement of Pde6g in the regulation of cGMP hydrolysis across different pathways supports the hypothesis that Vardenafil and Pde6g are functionally linked through this mechanism.",
